{
  "title": "Does the green checkout button sell more?",
  "setting": "Week 2 at Sunburst Gear",
  "mission": "Decide whether to **launch the new green checkout button** to all shoppers or keep testing.",
  "deadline": "Release decision with {{pm}} today at 4:00 p.m.",
  "panels": [
    {
      "title": "Checkout A/B Test — First 6 Hours",
      "metrics": [
        {
          "label": "Blue button (A)",
          "value": "4.0%",
          "note": "8 purchases out of 200 visits"
        },
        {
          "label": "Green button (B)",
          "value": "6.5%",
          "note": "13 purchases out of 200 visits"
        }
      ],
      "note": "400 total visits since 9:00 a.m. · Average order is $50.00"
    }
  ],
  "team": [
    {
      "id": "data",
      "role": "Senior data analyst",
      "expertise": "Cares about clean experiments and avoiding false alarms.",
      "ai": false
    },
    {
      "id": "pm",
      "role": "Product manager",
      "expertise": "Wants to hit quarterly sales goals and move fast.",
      "ai": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ai",
      "role": "AI data assistant",
      "expertise": "Runs quick statistical checks and sample size estimates on request.",
      "ai": true
    }
  ],
  "meters": [
    {
      "id": "risk",
      "label": "Risk of a bad launch",
      "start": 50,
      "goodWhen": "low"
    },
    {
      "id": "trust",
      "label": "Team trust in your call",
      "start": 60,
      "goodWhen": "high"
    }
  ],
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "chance",
      "name": "Tell a real difference from chance",
      "practice": "Look at a small poll or daily sales chart and write down one reason a 5-item gap could happen by pure luck."
    },
    {
      "id": "sample",
      "name": "Choose a sample size",
      "practice": "Before checking a two-group comparison, state how many observations you will wait for so you do not stop at a lucky spike."
    },
    {
      "id": "explain",
      "name": "Explain a test result",
      "practice": "Rewrite one sentence containing 'p-value' or 'statistical significance' in plain words a store manager would use."
    }
  ],
  "startNodeId": "morning-check",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "morning-check",
      "messages": [
        {
          "from": "pm",
          "text": "Look at the live dashboard! The green button is at 6.5% and the old blue one is at 4.0%. Let's push green to everyone before the afternoon rush."
        },
        {
          "from": "data",
          "text": "Hold on. That is only 13 sales versus 8 sales. In **hypothesis testing**, we start with the **null hypothesis**—the baseline idea that both buttons actually perform the same and this 5-sale gap is just random luck."
        }
      ],
      "prompt": "How do you check whether the 6.5% vs. 4.0% gap is real?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "id": "ask-ai-check",
          "text": "Ask {{ai}} how often a 13 vs. 8 split happens by pure chance, and how many visits we need.",
          "quality": "strong",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "ai",
              "text": "If both buttons truly convert at the same rate, a gap this big or bigger happens by random chance about 26% of the time (p-value = 0.26). To reliably spot a real 1% lift, you need 3,000 visits per button."
            },
            {
              "from": "data",
              "text": "A 26% chance of a fluke is way too high. Usually we want that **p-value**—the chance of seeing a gap this big by luck alone—to be under 5% before we call a winner."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "risk",
              "change": -20
            },
            {
              "meter": "trust",
              "change": 15
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": null,
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "chance",
              "kind": "good",
              "text": "You checked whether a 5-purchase gap in 400 visits could be random luck before acting on it.",
              "example": null
            }
          ],
          "next": "explain-to-pm"
        },
        {
          "id": "ship-now",
          "text": "Agree with {{pm}} that 6.5% is more than 50% higher than 4.0% and recommend launching green now.",
          "quality": "weak",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "pm",
              "text": "Love the speed. Let's get the engineering ticket ready for 4:00 p.m."
            },
            {
              "from": "data",
              "text": "Wait—I just ran the numbers with {{ai}}. With only 200 visits per group, a 13 vs. 8 split happens by pure luck 26% of the time! We need 3,000 visits per button to be sure."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "risk",
              "change": 25
            },
            {
              "meter": "trust",
              "change": -15
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": null,
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "chance",
              "kind": "improve",
              "text": "You judged the win by the percentage jump alone without checking how easily 5 extra sales can happen by chance.",
              "example": "Ask {{ai}} how often an 8 vs. 13 split happens by luck before calling a winner."
            }
          ],
          "next": "explain-to-pm"
        },
        {
          "id": "wait-till-four",
          "text": "Suggest waiting until 3:30 p.m. to add a couple hundred more visits before deciding.",
          "quality": "fair",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "ai",
              "text": "At current traffic, waiting until 3:30 p.m. adds 150 visits per button. Right now, a gap this size happens by luck 26% of the time; to reliably spot a real lift, you need 3,000 visits per button."
            },
            {
              "from": "data",
              "text": "Good instinct to wait, though a few more hours won't get us to 3,000 visits per group."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "risk",
              "change": -5
            },
            {
              "meter": "trust",
              "change": 5
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": null,
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "sample",
              "kind": "improve",
              "text": "You paused the rush, but picked a time cutoff instead of checking how many visits the test actually needs.",
              "example": "Ask {{ai}} how many visits per button are needed to detect a real change."
            }
          ],
          "next": "explain-to-pm"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "explain-to-pm",
      "messages": [
        {
          "from": "pm",
          "text": "I'm not a stats person. What does this 26% number actually mean for our store, and why can't we just take the extra sales today?"
        }
      ],
      "prompt": "How do you explain the test result and sample size to {{pm}}?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "id": "jargon-explanation",
          "text": "Tell him the p-value is 0.26, so we fail to reject the null hypothesis at alpha = 0.05 until n = 3,000.",
          "quality": "weak",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "pm",
              "text": "You lost me at 'fail to reject the null.' Look, if you can't give me a business reason, let's just run it for one more day and decide tomorrow."
            },
            {
              "from": "data",
              "text": "He meant that 5 extra sales can easily be a random morning fluke. Let's see how much traffic we get."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "risk",
              "change": 10
            },
            {
              "meter": "trust",
              "change": -15
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": {
            "label": "24 hours later (1,000 visits per button)",
            "panel": {
              "title": "Checkout A/B Test — Day 2 Snapshot",
              "metrics": [
                {
                  "label": "Blue button (A)",
                  "value": "4.2%",
                  "note": "42 purchases out of 1,000 visits"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Green button (B)",
                  "value": "4.7%",
                  "note": "47 purchases out of 1,000 visits"
                }
              ],
              "note": "2,000 total visits · Target sample size is 6,000 total visits"
            }
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "explain",
              "kind": "improve",
              "text": "You used textbook terms like 'p-value' and 'reject the null' with a colleague who asked for plain language.",
              "example": "\"Even if both buttons are identical, random luck creates a 5-sale gap about 1 time in 4. Let's wait for 3,000 visits per button.\""
            }
          ],
          "next": "mid-test-check"
        },
        {
          "id": "plain-full-sample",
          "text": "Explain that a 5-sale lead happens 1 in 4 times by pure luck, and ask for 5 days to reach 3,000 visits per button.",
          "quality": "strong",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "pm",
              "text": "Got it—1 in 4 is basically flipping two coins and getting two heads. Waiting 5 days for 3,000 visits each makes sense so we don't chase a mirage."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "risk",
              "change": -20
            },
            {
              "meter": "trust",
              "change": 20
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": {
            "label": "5 days later (3,000 visits per button)",
            "panel": {
              "title": "Checkout A/B Test — Full 6,000 Visits",
              "metrics": [
                {
                  "label": "Blue button (A)",
                  "value": "4.3%",
                  "note": "129 purchases out of 3,000 visits"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Green button (B)",
                  "value": "4.2%",
                  "note": "126 purchases out of 3,000 visits"
                }
              ],
              "note": "p-value = 0.85 · Difference is -0.1 percentage points"
            }
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "explain",
              "kind": "good",
              "text": "You translated the 26% p-value into an everyday '1 in 4 chance of luck' and tied it to the 3,000-visit target.",
              "example": null
            },
            {
              "skill": "sample",
              "kind": "good",
              "text": "You committed to the full 3,000 visits per group instead of peeking every few hours.",
              "example": null
            }
          ],
          "next": "final-call-full"
        },
        {
          "id": "compromise-one-day",
          "text": "Say that 200 visits is too small a sample, and propose checking again tomorrow at 1,000 visits per button.",
          "quality": "fair",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "pm",
              "text": "Okay, I can give you until tomorrow afternoon. Let's look at the numbers once we hit 1,000 visits each."
            },
            {
              "from": "data",
              "text": "1,000 is better than 200, though remember {{ai}} said we really need 3,000 per group."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "risk",
              "change": -5
            },
            {
              "meter": "trust",
              "change": 5
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": {
            "label": "24 hours later (1,000 visits per button)",
            "panel": {
              "title": "Checkout A/B Test — Day 2 Snapshot",
              "metrics": [
                {
                  "label": "Blue button (A)",
                  "value": "4.2%",
                  "note": "42 purchases out of 1,000 visits"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Green button (B)",
                  "value": "4.7%",
                  "note": "47 purchases out of 1,000 visits"
                }
              ],
              "note": "2,000 total visits · Target sample size is 6,000 total visits"
            }
          },
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "sample",
              "kind": "improve",
              "text": "You bargained down to 1,000 visits per group even though the test needed 3,000 to spot a real difference.",
              "example": "Explain why 3,000 visits per button is needed so the team doesn't stop one-third of the way through."
            }
          ],
          "next": "mid-test-check"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mid-test-check",
      "messages": [
        {
          "from": "pm",
          "text": "We're at 1,000 visits each now. Green is still ahead, 4.7% to 4.2%. That's two days in a row! Can we call it a winner now?"
        },
        {
          "from": "ai",
          "text": "At 1,000 visits per group, 47 vs. 42 purchases gives a p-value of 0.59. If both buttons are identical, a 5-sale gap happens by luck 59% of the time."
        }
      ],
      "prompt": "What do you recommend at 1,000 visits per button?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "id": "hold-for-3000",
          "text": "Show {{pm}} that the lead shrank to 5 sales total—basically a coin flip—and finish the remaining 2,000 visits per button.",
          "quality": "strong",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "pm",
              "text": "Whoa, I didn't notice it's literally the exact same 5-sale lead we had yesterday morning. Let's wait for the full 3,000 visits."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "risk",
              "change": -20
            },
            {
              "meter": "trust",
              "change": 15
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": null,
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "sample",
              "kind": "good",
              "text": "You held the line for the full 3,000-visit sample size and pointed out how the early percentage lead shrank as more shoppers arrived.",
              "example": null
            }
          ],
          "next": "ending-waited-recovered"
        },
        {
          "id": "ship-at-1000",
          "text": "Call green the winner since it stayed ahead for two days and {{pm}} wants to move on.",
          "quality": "weak",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "pm",
              "text": "Great, shipping green to 100% of shoppers this afternoon!"
            },
            {
              "from": "data",
              "text": "I'm logging that we stopped at one-third of our planned sample with a 59% chance the gap is noise."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "risk",
              "change": 30
            },
            {
              "meter": "trust",
              "change": -20
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": null,
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "chance",
              "kind": "improve",
              "text": "You treated 'ahead two days in a row' as proof, even though the 5-sale gap was still well within random chance (59%).",
              "example": "\"Green gained zero net lead since yesterday morning—let's finish the 3,000 visits per button.\""
            }
          ],
          "next": "ending-rushed"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "final-call-full",
      "messages": [
        {
          "from": "data",
          "text": "We hit 3,000 visits per button! Blue is at 4.3% (129 sales) and Green is at 4.2% (126 sales)."
        },
        {
          "from": "pm",
          "text": "Wild—that huge Day 1 lead completely vanished. So is Blue actually better than Green now, or what do I tell the design team?"
        }
      ],
      "prompt": "How do you wrap up the test for {{pm}} and the design team?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "id": "call-it-a-tie",
          "text": "Explain that a 3-sale difference over 6,000 visits is just random noise, so both colors perform the same and we can keep Blue.",
          "quality": "strong",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "pm",
              "text": "Crystal clear. We won't waste engineering time swapping the button color, and we'll test a bigger checkout change next week."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "risk",
              "change": -15
            },
            {
              "meter": "trust",
              "change": 15
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": null,
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "explain",
              "kind": "good",
              "text": "You clearly explained that when the test finishes with a tiny, high-p-value gap, the takeaway is 'no meaningful difference.'",
              "example": null
            }
          ],
          "next": "ending-clean"
        },
        {
          "id": "declare-blue-winner",
          "text": "Tell the team the test proved Blue beats Green by 0.1 percentage points because 129 sales is higher than 126.",
          "quality": "weak",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "data",
              "text": "Careful—with a p-value of 0.85, that 3-sale gap is pure random noise too. We shouldn't tell design that Blue 'beat' Green, just that they tied."
            },
            {
              "from": "pm",
              "text": "Good catch. Either way, we'll stick with our current Blue button."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "risk",
              "change": 5
            },
            {
              "meter": "trust",
              "change": -10
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": null,
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "chance",
              "kind": "improve",
              "text": "You called Blue superior based on a 3-sale difference that had an 85% chance of being random luck.",
              "example": "\"A 3-sale gap in 6,000 visits is random noise, so both colors perform the same.\""
            }
          ],
          "next": "ending-clean"
        },
        {
          "id": "extend-another-week",
          "text": "Suggest running the test for another two weeks to see if Blue's 0.1% edge turns into a real win.",
          "quality": "fair",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "data",
              "text": "Chasing a 0.1% difference would take hundreds of thousands of visits. At 3,000 visits per group, we already know Green didn't give us a meaningful lift."
            },
            {
              "from": "pm",
              "text": "Yeah, let's keep Blue and move our testing traffic to a bigger idea."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "risk",
              "change": 0
            },
            {
              "meter": "trust",
              "change": -5
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": null,
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "sample",
              "kind": "improve",
              "text": "You proposed extending the test after already reaching the planned sample size needed to rule out a meaningful lift.",
              "example": "\"We hit our 6,000-visit target and saw no real lift, so let's keep Blue and test a new idea.\""
            }
          ],
          "next": "ending-clean"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "endings": [
    {
      "id": "ending-clean",
      "outcome": "After all 6,000 visits, Green (4.2%) and Blue (4.3%) finished in a statistical tie. You kept the existing Blue button, saved engineering a pointless release, and moved on to testing a faster checkout form."
    },
    {
      "id": "ending-waited-recovered",
      "outcome": "Holding out for all 6,000 visits paid off: Green's early 6.5% spike faded to 4.2% against Blue's 4.3%. {{pm}} thanked you for stopping the team from celebrating a Day 1 fluke."
    },
    {
      "id": "ending-rushed",
      "outcome": "You launched the Green button early at 1,000 visits per group. Over the next week, overall checkout conversion settled at 4.2%—no better than the old Blue button—and the team had to walk back the victory announcement."
    }
  ],
  "summary": [
    "In a small sample, a big percentage gap can easily happen by random luck alone.",
    "A p-value tells you how often a difference at least as big as yours would appear by chance if both versions were truly equal.",
    "Set your target sample size before the test starts and explain results in plain odds instead of statistical jargon."
  ]
}

{
  "title": "E se o nosso mundo cotidiano funcionasse em pacotes quânticos?",
  "setting": "Cozinha de casa · Tarde de sábado",
  "mission": "Prepare um café da tarde e **descubra como a energia em pacotes e a luz como onda e partícula mudam a sua cozinha**.",
  "deadline": null,
  "panels": [
    {
      "title": "Forno em modo quântico",
      "metrics": [
        {
          "label": "Temperatura atual",
          "value": "0 °C",
          "note": "Desligado"
        },
        {
          "label": "Salto mínimo",
          "value": "100 °C",
          "note": "1 pacote (quantum)"
        }
      ],
      "note": "A receita do pão de queijo pede 180 °C, e a janela tem duas frestas estreitas."
    }
  ],
  "team": [
    {
      "id": "fisica",
      "role": "Amiga física",
      "expertise": "Adora experimentos caseiros e vê física em tudo",
      "ai": false
    },
    {
      "id": "vizinho",
      "role": "Vizinho curioso",
      "expertise": "Veio pelo cheiro de café e quer comer logo",
      "ai": false
    }
  ],
  "meters": [
    {
      "id": "sucesso",
      "label": "Sucesso do lanche",
      "start": 50,
      "goodWhen": "high"
    }
  ],
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "pacotes",
      "name": "Energia em pacotes",
      "practice": "Encontre dois exemplos no seu dia a dia de coisas que só vêm em unidades inteiras, como degraus de escada ou moedas, e compare com uma rampa."
    },
    {
      "id": "dualidade",
      "name": "Onda e partícula",
      "practice": "Explique para alguém em uma frase como a luz pode se espalhar como onda no ar, mas bater como pequenos grãos em uma superfície."
    }
  ],
  "startNodeId": "forno",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "forno",
      "messages": [
        {
          "from": "vizinho",
          "text": "Trouxe o pão de queijo! A embalagem diz para assar a 180 °C. Vou girar o botão do forno bem devagarinho até chegar lá."
        },
        {
          "from": "fisica",
          "text": "Espera! Hoje a sua cozinha está no modo quântico. Aqui a energia só vem em pacotes inteiros chamados _quanta_, como degraus de uma escada, sem meio-termo."
        }
      ],
      "prompt": "O forno só salta de 100 °C em 100 °C. Como você assa o pão de queijo?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "id": "dois-pacotes",
          "text": "Colocar no nível de 2 pacotes (200 °C) e tirar a forma um pouco antes para não queimar.",
          "quality": "strong",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "fisica",
              "text": "Boa saída! Como não existe pacote quebrado para dar 180 °C, você usou dois pacotes inteiros e compensou no tempo."
            },
            {
              "from": "vizinho",
              "text": "Olha só, o botão deu dois cliques secos: 100 °C, depois 200 °C! Nem sinal dos números do meio."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "sucesso",
              "change": 25
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": null,
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "pacotes",
              "kind": "good",
              "text": "Você trabalhou com os pacotes inteiros disponíveis em vez de tentar um valor intermediário proibido.",
              "example": null
            }
          ],
          "next": "janela"
        },
        {
          "id": "um-pacote",
          "text": "Usar apenas 1 pacote (100 °C) e deixar o dobro do tempo para assar com calma.",
          "quality": "fair",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "vizinho",
              "text": "Está demorando um pouco e ficando meio branquelo, mas pelo menos não vai queimar."
            },
            {
              "from": "fisica",
              "text": "Um pacote só dá pouca energia por vez, mas já respeita a regra de pular em degraus inteiros."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "sucesso",
              "change": 5
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": null,
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "pacotes",
              "kind": "good",
              "text": "Você respeitou a regra dos pacotes inteiros, mesmo escolhendo um degrau de energia baixo para a receita.",
              "example": null
            }
          ],
          "next": "janela"
        },
        {
          "id": "travar-botao",
          "text": "Segurar o botão com fita adesiva bem no meio, entre o 1 e o 2, para forçar os 180 °C.",
          "quality": "weak",
          "replies": [
            {
              "from": "vizinho",
              "text": "Ué, o botão escorregou sozinho de volta para o 100 °C! Não fica no meio de jeito nenhum."
            },
            {
              "from": "fisica",
              "text": "É como tentar parar no ar entre dois degraus da escada. Quando a energia é quantizada, o meio do caminho não existe."
            }
          ],
          "effects": [
            {
              "meter": "sucesso",
              "change": -20
            }
          ],
          "timeJump": null,
          "notes": [
            {
              "skill": "pacotes",
              "kind": "improve",
              "text": "Você tentou escolher um valor contínuo entre dois níveis, mas a energia quântica só aceita pacotes inteiros.",
              "example": "Escolha 1 pacote (100 °C) ou 2 pacotes (200 °C) e ajuste o tempo de forno."
            }
          ],
          "next": "janela"
        }
      ]
